Today marks the 16th anniversary of when Apple released the first-generation <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a>. After Steve Jobs announced the &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; on January 27, 2010, it launched a few months later on April 3, 2010.<br/>
<br/>
<img class="alignnone size-full wp-image-545693" src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2017/01/original-ipad-2.jpg" alt="" width="800" height="523" /><br/>
Apple's original &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; looked like a larger version of the iPhone, featuring thick bezels, a 9.7-inch multitouch display with a 1,024 x 768 resolution, a Home Button, and a 30-pin dock connector. The &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; was the first device that Apple released with a custom-designed chip, the Apple A4.<br/>
<br/>
The concept of a tablet device like the &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; actually predated the iPhone, and Apple had a prototype project in the works in 2004. Then-Apple CEO Steve Jobs wanted to prioritize the iPhone, so the &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; was put on hold until after the iPhone was established.<br/>
<br/>
Apple priced the &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; at &#36;499, and Jobs called it a "magical and revolutionary device at an unbelievable price." Like the iPhone, the &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; dominated the tablet space, and it still continues to do so today. Apple sold a million iPads in the first month, and while competitors like Microsoft and Samsung quickly came out with competing tablets of their own, the &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; has no match.<br/>
<br/>
Apple has led the global tablet market for over a decade and no competitor comes close to matching <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/02/05/ipad-demand-surges/">its tablet market share</a>. As of the fourth quarter of 2025, Apple's market share was 44.9 percent, with Samsung the next closest at 14.7 percent.<br/>
<br/>
